      "textEn": "73\n\nMeaning of the word\n\nTam—of Durvasa; Anvadhavat—started following; Bhagavat-ratha-angam—the wheel that came out of the chariot wheel of the Lord; Dava-agni—like the fire of the forest; Uddhut—rising; Shikha—coils; Yatha Ahim—the way snakes chase; Tatha—in the same way; Anushaktam—as if touching the back of Durvasa Muni; Muni—Muni; Ikshmam—looking; Guham—in the cave;\nVivikshmah—wanted to enter; Prasar—started moving quickly; Merah—of Mount Meru; Just as the flames of a fire chase a snake, similarly, the wheel of God began to chase Durvasa Muni. Durvasa Muni saw that this chakra was about to touch his back, therefore he quickly\nran and wanted to enter the cave of Sumeru mountain. He who went to the directions, the sky, the earth, the ocean,\nTo the people, the world, the stars, and the divine.\nWherever he runs, there he is.\nHe is seen as the best of good, the unpunctual, and the unpleasant. 51. Meaning of the word:\n\nDirection: All directions; Sky: In the sky; Earth: On the earth; Pits: In the pits; Seas: Inside the seas; Places: In all places; And with them: Including their rulers; In the three worlds: In the heavens; Gone: Went; He: That Durvasa Muni; From where: From where; Where: Where-\nWhere; Driven: Went; There: There; Sudarshanam: The Chakra of the Lord; Dushpasaham: Extremely frightening; Durdarsaham: Unseen; Durdarsa Muni saw. To protect himself, Durvasa Muni kept running everywhere—he went to the sky, the earth, caves,\nthe sea, the rulers of the three worlds, to various worlds, and even to the heavens, but wherever he went,\nthere he saw the unbearable fire of the Sudarshan Chakra chasing him. Alabdhanatha: Always in a state of restlessness.\nSantrastachitto aranameeshamaanah.\nThe gods, the lord of the universe, the knower of the self, the unconquered, the radiant one, I am not. 52. Meaning of the word:\n\nUnavailable-Nath: Without taking refuge in the protector; Sah: Durvasa Muni; Sada: Always; Kutashchit: Somewhere; Santras